      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "frame",
      "id": "c7mt9",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2447,
      "name": "Admin / Brief / Error band",
      "clip": true,
      "width": 640,
      "fill": "$surface-container",
      "layout": "vertical",
      "gap": 22,
      "padding": 36,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "uacJI",
          "name": "Flow title",
          "fill": "$on-surface",
          "content": "Admin — what it says when it cannot answer",
          "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
          "fontSize": 24,
          "fontWeight": "700"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "NI30P",
          "name": "Kit pin",
          "fill": "$outline",
          "content": "kit-version-source 0.3.0 · band 2 of 2 · error and alternate paths · 9 of 9 screens drawn",
          "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
          "fontSize": 10,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "wgn7n",
          "name": "Purpose",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "Lu04E",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Purpose",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "toLVl",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Everything the admin says when a read fails, a write is refused, a limit is hit, or there is genuinely nothing to show. Band 1 is the happy path; this band is the rest of the truth. Its subject is copy, not layout — with one exception the screens are band 1's and what changes is the sentence in the slot. The rule the whole band exists to hold is the one D15 and D16 were both about: loading, unreachable and genuinely empty are three different states and must never collapse into one screen. Read left to right in nav order, the front door first, then the five pages behind it.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "NpVZu",
          "name": "Entry points",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "qZ2cL",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Entry points",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "zVw4j",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "None of its own. Every screen here is a state of the band 1 screen above it, reached by the same route; what differs is what the gateway answered. Sign in's two panels come back on the same POST /admin/login that draws the form, rendered by the server (src/admin/login.js:135 and :141) — which is why they still appear when the dashboard's own JavaScript cannot run. The dashboard states are client-side: every admin read reports into one connection store (public/js/app.js:186), so a single failed read raises the Not updating banner over whichever page is open.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "p5oitz",
          "name": "Screens in this band",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "GKuhG",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Screens in this band",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "iRMoJ",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Nine, at 514a8f8. Sign in / Invalid credentials and Sign in / Rate limited are the 401 and 429 panels D43 deliberately left for this band; both fields come back empty, because the server re-renders the form without value attributes. Overview / Not updating — nothing answered, so the page collapses to its header, the NOW row and one line. Overview / Usage limit — every read worked and the quota did not; it is the only screen here at full height and the only one that keeps the fold marker. Request Logs / Unreachable — the stats strip, both panels at '-' and the table's error row, with pagination still at Page 1 of 1 and a toolbar down to Refresh alone, since Export CSV and Clear All are both x-show total > 0. API Keys / Empty is the one screen in the band where nothing went wrong: a read that worked and found no keys. Its activity log is drawn expanded, one click from the default — showAudit is false (public/js/keys.js:16) — because that panel's empty line is copy that renders nowhere else. Playground / Waiting and / No provider carry between them the three catalog states D16 closed, plus the waiting clock; their MODEL and FORMAT values are the operator's, not the app's, which opens on claude and OpenAI · /v1/chat/completions. System / Unreachable — the build card unread and the providers line.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "NrZsQ",
          "name": "Business rules",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "k5CKtF",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Business rules",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "gqDqO",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Three states, never two: loading, unreachable and genuinely empty get different sentences everywhere all three can happen. That is why API Keys / Empty sits in this band beside screens that failed — it is the third state, not a failure. The banner is shell-level and conditional: it lives in main above every page (views/index.html:148), so it is drawn identically on four screens here and belongs to none of them. Degraded and unreachable are not the same question — the banner reads the last read of any request, while the sidebar and footer read the health poll, which only runs every 30s (app.js:239). Playground / Waiting is where they disagree on purpose: the banner reports a 500 on the model catalog while the sidebar still says UP, because health itself is fine. Measured in a browser on 2026-09-20 and it does happen, with two caveats the artboard cannot show — it is order-dependent, because reportRead is last-write-wins and a failure that resolves before the health read is erased by it; and it is transient, because the next poll clears the banner on its own. On the three screens where health itself failed there is no read timestamp anywhere: lastReadAt is written only by a read that succeeded (app.js:34), so a page that never got an answer has nothing to stamp, and its footer is the product name alone. Sign in refuses on the server: 5 attempts per IP per 5 minutes (src/middleware/admin-auth.js:9,11), and the seconds in the 429 are the real remainder, not a fixed lockout.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "LHnJj",
          "name": "Copy source",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "BNIGt",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Copy source",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "YvDAs",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Every string here exists in the code and was copied from it. src/admin/login.js:135 and :141 for the two panels, and that file's own .error rule for the panel itself — a 3px left border in error over --error-bg, which is the same value as surface-container-lowest, at .8rem. views/index.html:148-154 for the banner, :123 for UNREACHABLE. overview.html:77-81 (USAGE LIMIT HIT), :194-197 (providers), :334-336 (stats). logs.html:70 and :125. keys.html:47 and :146. playground.html:56-58 and :81-83. system.html:234-239, plus public/js/system.js:254 for the refused toggle. The banner's two sentences are app.js:193-194. Numbers are illustrative; sentences never are. Verified against the running app on 2026-09-20, which is how four of these screens were corrected instead of shipped wrong: System drew a release line the code cannot reach on that screen (fetchLatestRelease returns early when the build is unknown, system.js:99-101), Logs drew two toolbar buttons the real page hides, three read timestamps could not coexist with a body that never got a read, and the sign-in password field was drawn filled. The catalog failure is drawn as a 500 rather than a 503 for the same reason: /admin/providers answers 200 or, on a throw, Express's 500 — it has no path that produces a 503, which belongs to /v1.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "bQwbM",
          "name": "States not drawn",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "aHhg2",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "States not drawn",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "gSMb0",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Five sentences that are a pure string swap into a slot this band already draws, kept here with their copy instead of given an artboard: 'Loading stats...' and 'Loading providers...' (overview.html:334, :194), 'Loading...' (logs.html:125, keys.html:47), 'Loading…' with the other ellipsis (system.html:234), and 'No providers configured.' (overview.html:197, system.html:239). Two are real states with a home already on the canvas: System's refused toggle, 'claude was not changed — the gateway answered 403.' in status-fail under the providers list (system.js:254, the D17 fix), and the offline flavour of the banner, 'This browser is offline. Nothing below is being updated.', which swaps only the middle sentence. Sign in's 501, 'SHELLM_ADMIN_PASSWORD not configured', is not drawn because it is a JSON error page rather than this form. The CRT texture note stays on band 1's Sign in artboard alone — these two states inherit the same untileable layers and do not repeat the note.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "x1T8y4",
          "name": "Open findings",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "XARg2",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "Open findings",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "uB9oj",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "D20 and D18 remain, and both are visible here: the two sign-in states are drawn from that page's own nine aliases, and every mono measurement is Roboto Mono standing in for a system stack. D46 was this band's own and is already closed — drawing System / Unreachable is what found it, because the canvas put the Concurrency card and the sidebar side by side and the contradiction was the picture: four zeros for a health read that never happened, beside a sidebar saying UNREACHABLE. The code now guards Running, Waiting and Streams on healthRead === 'ok', and Open circuits on the providers read instead, since openCircuits filters providers and not health. So this artboard draws four em dashes and documents the fix rather than the defect — it is the one screen in the band that was re-drawn after the code moved. D45 is closed too: band 1 and this band went to $text-sm and $text-lg in one pass, Logs' 25 fixed-width cells were checked first and again after, and no cell wrapped and no artboard moved.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "u99K8",
          "name": "History",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "m4KeR",
              "name": "Heading",
              "fill": "$primary-container",
              "content": "History",
              "fontFamily": "$font-headline",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "Rsmwz",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$on-surface-variant",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "See the Log frame below this band.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font-mono-render",
              "fontSize": 10,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
